# ship-pr's build of the would-be merge commit (lukstafi/ludics-lite#611, landed by
# lukstafi/ludics-lite#630). `pr-review.sh merge` reads this file from master: on EVERY merge
# (`trigger always`, below) it builds the PR head merged with master's tip in a scratch
# repository, runs `command` at its root inside the box's run-time slot, and refuses the merge
# (exit 1, no waiver) when that fails. Without this file a merge builds nothing.
#
# Trigger. `always` makes this build the merge gate: it runs whatever the two sides changed, a
# master that has not moved included, where the default (`same-input`) built only when a path
# both sides changed was an input. Wave-20261111 paid a 25 to 40 minute remote CI cycle for every
# head move; what this build reads locally, in about three minutes, is what lets those jobs leave
# `pull_request`. A reader older than lukstafi/ludics#1118 does not know the word: it refuses the
# config where both sides changed something and merges UNBUILT where one side changed nothing
# (lukstafi/ludics#936). Every fleet box's skills checkout carried that reader on 2026-11-13,
# when this line was added (b09b945f on mac-studio, macbook-pro, rog-nv-linux, minix-amd-linux
# and tuf-amd-linux). A box set up later must carry it before it merges here.
#
# What it builds. tools/merge-build-check.sh runs four tiers on the merged tree, the first failure
# refusing the merge: the formatter gate (CI's `Formatting` job), `dune build @check
# @test/operations/scans`, the per-test aliases the PR's change reaches (the pre-push list,
# without its `slow-` and `train-` members), and the shell harnesses that change reaches, of
# every group. The script's header says what each tier reads and what a
# cross-cutting change is owed instead.
#
# Inputs. Under `trigger always` no input decides a build. The line stays for the trigger a
# narrower future might name, and tools/merge-build-replay.py reads it to size one. What it
# meant under `same-input`, and still says about the command's reach: a path is an input when
# one glob matches the WHOLE repository path
# (fnmatch, case-sensitive, `*` crossing `/`); a shared path renamed on either side counts by its
# other name too. The one glob below makes EVERY path an input (gh-ocannl-1382): the command's
# compile tier runs `@test/operations/scans`, which read the repository itself -- Markdown notes
# and docs (agent_notes_structure, agents_md_size, doc_examples), goldens, workflow YAML, Python,
# scripts -- so a narrower list would be a second copy of the scans' corpora, stale the day a scan
# reads a new kind of file; the few paths nothing reads (editor settings) cost a build only when
# both sides change one. Replayed over the 474 first-parent merges on master from 2026-09-19 to
# 2026-10-19, the OCaml-and-scripts list this replaced built 166 of them, adding `*.md` would
# build 238 and every path builds 245, at about 100 s each (tools/merge-build-replay.py replays a
# history against any glob sets, these lines by default). What stays outside the build under any
# trigger: what the command does not run -- a test the change does not reach, a `slow-` or
# `train-` alias, a GPU backend; those are the nightly CI run's and the integration loop's, on
# master.
#
# Bound. `timeout` covers the command's slowest honest run: the format and compile tiers, the
# widest list the tests tier can be handed, and the WHOLE harness set, which every fail-closed
# fallback of tools/merge-build-check.sh runs, its members four at once (gh-ocannl-2002).
# Measured on 2026-11-13, each tier from a fresh clone, on mac-studio (load 5 to 8) and on
# minix-amd-linux (idle): format 4 s and 3 s; compile 136 s and 137 s; the tests tier on the 411
# aliases of `arrayjit/lib/low_level.ml`'s first tier, the widest list `affected` still calls
# narrow, 661 s and 731 s; the whole harness set, 35 members, 499 s (at load 5 to 11) and 347 s.
# Unbounded, that is 1300 s and 1218 s, so the tests tier is capped: it builds at most 200 aliases (TESTS_MAX in the script),
# about 320 s and 360 s at the measured 1.6 s and 1.8 s an alias, and a longer list is owed to
# the PR's own run. With the two asks of `affected` a cross-cutting change costs (about 50 s
# each), the slowest honest run is then about 1060 s and 950 s. 1800 s is 1.7 times that, and nine
# times the ordinary run: a merge whose change reaches some tens of tests and no harness took
# 200 s and 179 s whole (ship-pr's dry run, `pr-review.sh merge-build`). It is also the 30
# minutes no CI job's cap goes past. A bound near a measured pass is what overran before: 1200 s
# over a one-at-a-time shell group's 1167 s, at load 3.6 (gh-ocannl-1994). Re-measure when the
# harness set or the per-alias cost grows: tools/merge-build-check.sh's header names the tiers,
# and docs/agent-notes/build-and-test.md how each was timed.
#
# Trust. The command is this repository's own code, trusted as a developer's build is: the
# scratch repository is a place to build, not a sandbox. It runs from whatever environment
# `merge` has; tools/merge-build-check.sh states its tiers, what selects them, their measured
# cost and how it finds dune when that environment lacks the opam switch.
command tools/merge-build-check.sh
input *
trigger always
timeout 1800
